# Judgment, Decision Making, and Reasoning. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "What are decisions, and what is reasoning?", "Decisions are the process of making choices between alternatives, and reasoning is the process of drawing conclusions."),
 ([2], "How are decisions related to judgments?", "Decisions are based on the judgments people make. A judgment can contribute to a decision through an evidence-based inference, with the conclusion expressing what the observations support and the decision concerning what to do."),
 ([2], "What is inductive reasoning?", "Inductive reasoning is one of the primary mechanisms in making judgments. It is reasoning based on observations and reaching conclusions from evidence."),
 ([3], "How certain are inductive conclusions?", "They are probably, but not definitely, true, because the observations support an inference rather than guarantee every case."),
 ([3], "What does the strength of an inductive argument depend on?", "The representativeness, number and quality of the observations."),
 ([3], "What does a single black swan among many white swans show?", "Numerous white swans support an expectation about swan color, but a single clearly observed black swan contradicts the claim that every swan is white, so one high-quality observation can carry substantial weight."),
 ([4], "How is induction used in science and everyday life?", "Scientific discoveries use induction when observations support hypotheses and general conclusions. In everyday life people predict what will happen from what has happened in the past, especially in familiar situations."),
 ([4], "Does inductive reasoning always involve deliberate argument?", "No. Some inductive reasoning happens automatically, because a familiar pattern already supports an expectation."),
 ([5], "What are heuristics?", "Heuristics are shortcuts that help people reach conclusions rapidly using past experience to guide present behavior. They are rules of thumb likely to provide a correct answer but not foolproof."),
 ([5], "How do the availability and representativeness heuristics differ?", "The availability heuristic uses how readily examples come to mind, and the representativeness heuristic uses how much a case resembles a category."),
 ([6], "What is the availability heuristic?", "It makes events that are more easily remembered seem more probable than events that are harder to remember. The more examples a person can think of, the more probable the event is judged to be."),
 ([6], "What makes an event more available without making it more common?", "Distinctiveness, emotional salience and frequent encoding or retrieval."),
 ([7], "What is the word judgment about the letter r?", "It asks whether English has more words that begin with r or more words with r as the third letter."),
 ([7], "What do most people answer, and what is correct?", "About 70 percent choose words beginning with r, but words with r in the third position are actually three times more common."),
 ([7], "Why does the availability heuristic produce the r-word error?", "Retrieving words by first letter is easier than searching for a letter in the third position, so words beginning with r come to mind readily and that set feels more numerous."),
 ([8], "What are the causes-of-death comparisons?", "People judge which of two causes would be more likely to kill a randomly selected person in the United States, such as homicide versus appendicitis, drowning versus an auto–train collision, asthma versus botulism, asthma versus a tornado and appendicitis versus pregnancy."),
 ([8], "Why do people pick the less likely cause of death?", "A death from botulism can become front-page news while deaths from asthma go virtually unnoticed, so distinctive coverage makes the unusual cause more available even though asthma is more likely."),
 ([8], "In the causes-of-death comparisons, which is more likely, asthma or botulism or a tornado?", "Asthma is more likely than botulism or a tornado, yet many respondents select the less likely cause."),
 ([9], "What is the representativeness heuristic?", "It bases a judgment on how much one event resembles another, estimating whether A belongs to class B from how well A’s properties resemble those normally associated with B."),
 ([9], "What is the Robert problem?", "Robert wears glasses, speaks quietly and reads a lot, and many people judge him more likely to be a librarian than a farmer."),
 ([9], "What is the base rate, and what is base-rate neglect?", "The base rate is the background prevalence of the categories, here about 2.2 million farms and 120,000 libraries in America. Base-rate neglect occurs when a similarity-based response displaces that information."),
 ([10], "What do people do with base rates in the lawyer–engineer problem when no description is given?", "A group has 70 lawyers and 30 engineers, so the chance of picking an engineer is 30 out of 100, and respondents generally use the base rate correctly."),
 ([10], "What happens when Jack is described?", "Jack is described as conservative, careful, ambitious, and enjoying home carpentry, sailing and mathematical puzzles, and respondents tend to call him an engineer. Descriptive information dominates even though the group still contains more lawyers."),
 ([11], "What is the conjunction rule?", "The probability of two events occurring together cannot be greater than the probability of either event alone, because adding a requirement restricts the set of cases."),
 ([11], "What is the Linda problem, and why is the common answer an error?", "Linda is described as outspoken, bright, trained in philosophy and concerned with social justice, and about 82 percent choose bank teller and active in the feminist movement over bank teller. Feminist bank tellers are inside the larger set of bank tellers, so the conjunction cannot be more probable."),
 ([12], "What is the hospital question?", "A larger hospital has about 45 births per day and a smaller one about 15, with about half of babies boys, and the question is which hospital records more days when over 60 percent of the babies are boys."),
 ([12], "Why is the answer about the same incorrect?", "That answer, chosen by 56 percent, treats the shared overall proportion as enough and leaves out the difference between observing a proportion in 15 births and in 45 births."),
 ([13], "Which hospital has more days with over 60 percent boys, and why?", "The smaller hospital. Smaller samples vary more from one observation to another, so an extreme proportion is more likely in 15 births than in 45."),
 ([13], "Why is choosing the larger hospital also a mistake?", "It confuses an absolute count with the frequency of an extreme proportion, because the question asks which hospital more often crosses 60 percent."),
 ([14], "What is the law of large numbers?", "As more individuals are randomly drawn from a population, the resulting group becomes more representative of the population."),
 ([14], "What mistake do people make about small samples?", "They often assume that representativeness holds for small samples too, which understates how much variation is possible when few observations are involved."),
 ([15], "What is an illusory correlation?", "It is a relationship that appears to exist even though it does not exist or is much weaker than assumed."),
 ([15], "What are stereotypes, and how do they shape attention?", "Stereotypes are oversimplified generalizations about a group, often emphasizing negative characteristics. They direct attention toward behaviors that fit the expected description."),
 ([15], "How are illusory correlations related to availability?", "Stereotype-consistent behaviors received particular attention and are easier to bring to mind, and their availability is mistaken for evidence of a strong association."),
 ([16], "What did Lord and colleagues (1979) do?", "People who favored capital punishment and people who opposed it read the same article."),
 ([16], "What did Lord and colleagues (1979) find?", "Those in favor interpreted the article as favorable to their position, and those against interpreted it as favorable to theirs, so existing attitude influenced how the evidence was evaluated."),
 ([16], "What is the myside bias?", "It is the tendency to generate and evaluate evidence, and to test hypotheses, in ways biased toward one’s own opinions and attitudes."),
 ([17], "What is confirmation bias?", "It is the tendency to seek information that fits a hypothesis and overlook information that argues against it. Myside bias is one form of it."),
 ([17], "How does the 2, 4, 6 task work?", "Participants receive 2, 4, 6 as a sequence that fits a hidden rule, write their own sets of three numbers with reasons, receive feedback only on whether each fits the rule, and then propose the rule."),
 ([17], "What is the actual rule in the 2, 4, 6 task, and what mistake is common?", "The rule is three numbers in increasing order. The most common hypothesis is increasing intervals of two, and testing only sequences that satisfy it gives repeated positive feedback without distinguishing it from the broader rule."),
 ([18], "What is utility?", "Utility refers to outcomes that are desirable because they serve the person’s interests, expressing how beneficial an outcome is to the decision maker."),
 ([18], "What is expected utility theory?", "It assumes that people are rational and, with all relevant information, choose the alternative with maximum expected utility, combining the value of outcomes with their chances. The monetary version uses maximum expected monetary payoff."),
 ([19], "Why does expected utility not fully describe what people choose?", "People find value in things other than money, and many decisions fail to maximize the probability of the best outcome, so people are not utility-maximization machines."),
 ([19], "What does casino gambling show?", "People continue to choose an activity despite knowing that the odds are strongly against them, so decision behavior involves considerations beyond maximum expected monetary payoff."),
 ([19], "What does expected utility theory offer for comparing alternatives?", "It supplies specific procedures for determining the best choice according to expected benefit, a consistent basis for comparing alternatives when their outcomes and probabilities are available."),
 ([20], "What are the two bowls in the jellybean problem?", "Participants earn one dollar when they draw a red jellybean and choose between a bowl with 1 red bean out of 10 and a bowl with 7 red beans out of 100."),
 ([20], "Which bowl do people choose, and what does it show?", "Most choose the 7-out-of-100 bowl even though the chance is 7 percent against 10 percent. The larger number of favorable items draws attention away from the denominator, so the decision depends on considerations other than actual probabilities."),
 ([21], "What happened to travel after September 11?", "Air travel decreased and driving increased, and the probability of dying in a car accident is much greater than while flying."),
 ([21], "What does the shift from flying to driving after September 11 show about risk decisions?", "Avoiding a feared event increased exposure to a more likely danger. The reported number of Americans who died on the road while avoiding flying exceeded the total number of passengers killed on the four hijacked flights."),
 ([22], "What is the convoy dilemma?", "A convoy of food trucks travels to a refugee camp during a famine, and rerouting saves 1,000 people at a second camp but 100 people in the first camp die because the trucks no longer arrive."),
 ([22], "What did Ritov and Baron (1999) find in the convoy dilemma?", "63 percent did not choose to reroute, even though rerouting has the better net count, 1,000 − 100 = 900. Some would keep the original route even if only one person at the first camp would die."),
 ([23], "What is the Sacred Value Protection Model of Tetlock, Kristel, Elson, Green and Lerner (2000)?", "It proposes that people want to protect what they hold sacred, and that sacred values are compartmentalized separately from secular values."),
 ([23], "What are taboo and tragic tradeoffs?", "A taboo tradeoff intermingles sacred and secular domains and can provoke moral outrage. A tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values with one another, and people are less morally outraged by it."),
 ([24], "What are the taboo and tragic scenarios with Johnny?", "In the taboo scenario, Robert can save Johnny, a five-year-old needing a liver transplant that costs one million dollars, or use the funds for equipment and salaries. In the tragic scenario, two children need a liver but only one is available."),
 ([25], "What did Tetlock and colleagues (2000) find about moral outrage?", "Under the taboo tradeoff, not saving Johnny produced much greater outrage than saving him, and under the tragic tradeoff outrage was similar whether Johnny was saved or not."),
 ([26], "Who participated in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), and what did they offer?", "They interviewed 601 Israeli settlers, 535 Palestinian refugees and 719 Palestinian students, and offered three types of tradeoff deals as possible solutions."),
 ([27], "What are the taboo, Taboo+ and symbolic deals in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "The taboo deal asks Palestinians to recognize the sacred and historic right of the Jewish people to Israel, with two states. Taboo+ adds one billion dollars a year for ten years from Israel. The symbolic version adds recognition of the Palestinians’ right to their own state and an apology."),
 ([28], "What are moral absolutists?", "They treat an issue under dispute as a sacred value and consider a compromise unacceptable regardless of how much benefit it could produce. Non-absolutists also have strong preferences but do not treat the issues as sacred."),
 ([28], "How common were sacred values in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "About 46 percent of the settlers held that they should never give up part of the Land of Israel, and about 54 percent of the Palestinian students treated both the right of return and Jerusalem as sacred."),
 ([29], "How did a material incentive affect absolutists and non-absolutists in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), for absolutists, adding money in Taboo+ increased anger, disgust and support for violence. For non-absolutists, it reduced them."),
 ([29], "How did the symbolic offer compare in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), symbolic recognition was associated with lower anger or disgust and lower support for violence, so material benefit and symbolic reciprocity are different."),
 ([29], "What does the comparison of material benefit and symbolic reciprocity in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007) show?", "In Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), increasing an economic incentive is not automatically an improvement in a negotiation over sacred concerns. What the offer asks people to exchange, and how it treats their commitments, affects the emotional and behavioral judgments they report."),
 ([30], "What are incentives and disincentives in negotiations over sacred values?", "An incentive offers a benefit for agreement, and a disincentive imposes a cost for refusing. Economic funds are an incentive and economic sanctions are a disincentive."),
 ([30], "What did Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009) investigate?", "They investigated whether people who treated Iran’s nuclear program as a sacred value would be amenable to material benefits or costs in exchange for compromise."),
 ([31], "How was a sacred value classified in the study by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), the measure, based on Baron and Spranca (1997), classified participants as holding a sacred value if they said giving up the program should not be done no matter how great the consequences."),
 ([31], "How big was the study by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), and how common were sacred values?", "In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), there were 1,997 Iranian participants, in Farsi. About 14 percent for nuclear energy and 13 percent for nuclear weapons chose the sacred-value response."),
 ([32], "What was the design of the experiment by Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010) used a 2 × 3 design, with the nuclear program being energy or weapons and the deal being Taboo, Taboo+ or Taboo−, giving six conditions."),
 ([32], "What are Taboo, Taboo+ and Taboo− in Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), Taboo has Iran give up its program and surrender facilities to the United Nations in return for Israel doing the same. Taboo+ adds 40 billion dollars from the United Nations to Iran, and Taboo− adds the threat of additional sanctions if Iran refuses."),
 ([33], "What happened for participants with a sacred value about nuclear energy in Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), approval was lower with the financial incentive and with sanctions than with the baseline, and anticipated anger was highest with the financial incentive and elevated with sanctions."),
 ([33], "How did participants without a sacred value respond in Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "In Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010), they had much higher approval and lower anger across the energy conditions, with little variation between the baseline, incentive and sanctions versions."),
]
R = [
 ("How do induction and heuristics differ as ways of reaching a judgment?", "Induction draws a probable conclusion from observed evidence, while heuristics are shortcuts that use past experience and cues like availability or similarity, and are likely correct but not foolproof.", [3,4,5]),
 ("How do the availability and representativeness heuristics each lead to errors?", "Availability substitutes ease of remembering for probability, as in the r-words and causes of death. Representativeness substitutes resemblance for probability, ignoring base rates in Robert and Jack and the conjunction rule in Linda.", [6,7,8,9,10,11]),
 ("Why do Robert and Jack show the same underlying error?", "In both, a description that resembles a category displaces the background frequencies of the categories, which is base-rate neglect.", [9,10]),
 ("How do the Linda problem and the base-rate problems both reflect representativeness?", "Each response follows how well a description fits a category, and not the probability relationship, the nested sets in Linda and the group composition in Jack.", [9,10,11]),
 ("How are the conjunction rule and the law of large numbers different kinds of probability principle?", "The conjunction rule concerns nested sets, since adding a condition cannot raise probability, while the law of large numbers concerns sample size and representativeness.", [11,12,13,14]),
 ("How do the hospital problem and small samples connect to representativeness?", "Answering about the same ignores the number of births behind each percentage, and people often assume small samples are representative, but small samples vary more and produce extreme proportions more often.", [12,13,14]),
 ("How do availability and illusory correlation connect?", "Stereotype-consistent behaviors get attention and are easier to bring to mind, and that availability is mistaken for strong evidence of association.", [6,15]),
 ("How do myside bias and confirmation bias differ?", "Confirmation bias is the broad tendency to seek fitting information and overlook the contrary, and myside bias is one form, applied to one’s own opinions and attitudes.", [16,17]),
 ("How do Lord and colleagues (1979) and the 2, 4, 6 task both show biased evidence use?", "In Lord and colleagues, the same article was read as favorable to each side. In the 2, 4, 6 task, testing only sequences that fit the hypothesis gives repeated positive feedback and leaves the narrow hypothesis intact.", [16,17]),
 ("Why would testing for disconfirmation help in the 2, 4, 6 task?", "Trying sequences that violate the hypothesis tests its boundaries, while confirming triples leave the narrower belief intact.", [17]),
 ("How do expected utility theory and the real choices of people differ?", "The theory says people choose the maximum expected utility, but people value things beyond money and gamble against the odds, so behavior departs from it.", [18,19,20]),
 ("How do the jellybean problem and the hospital problem both show people overlooking part of the information?", "In the jellybean problem the larger number of red beans drew attention away from the denominator, and in the hospital problem the answer about the same left out how many births were behind each day’s percentage.", [12,20]),
 ("What do the post-September 11 travel example and the causes-of-death comparisons each show about how decisions depart from actual probability?", "In the causes-of-death comparisons, distinctive coverage made an unusual cause more available than a more likely one. After September 11, the emotional impact of the attacks guided travel choices toward the more probable danger of the road.", [8,21]),
 ("How do the convoy dilemma of Ritov and Baron (1999) and expected utility show different ways of evaluating outcomes?", "In Ritov and Baron (1999), counting lives saved says reroute, but 63 percent did not, and reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved.", [18,22]),
 ("How do taboo and tragic tradeoffs differ in what is being compared?", "A taboo tradeoff compares a sacred value with a secular benefit, while a tragic tradeoff compares two sacred values.", [23,24]),
 ("What was the interaction in the Johnny results of Tetlock and colleagues (2000)?", "Outrage depended on the outcome only in the taboo condition, where not saving Johnny was far worse, and was similar for both outcomes in the tragic condition.", [24,25]),
 ("How do the Johnny scenarios of Tetlock and colleagues (2000) and the convoy dilemma of Ritov and Baron (1999) both show limits of counting outcomes?", "In the Johnny scenarios outrage depended on the type of tradeoff, and in the convoy dilemma reluctance to cause a loss can outweigh the larger number saved, so in both the responses were not a simple numerical comparison.", [22,23,24,25]),
 ("Why does a material incentive backfire for moral absolutists but not for non-absolutists in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "For absolutists the issue is sacred, so money makes an exchange they already treat as sacred more objectionable, raising anger, disgust and support for violence. For non-absolutists it lowers them.", [23,28,29]),
 ("Why does symbolic recognition work differently from money in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "It addresses the status of the group’s sacred claim, so it was associated with lower anger and lower support for violence, while money engages a material exchange.", [27,29]),
 ("How do Dehghani, Atran and colleagues (2010) and Dehghani, Iliev and colleagues (2009) share a design idea with Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007)?", "Both compare a taboo deal with versions that add material benefit or cost, and both separate participants who hold the issue sacred from those who do not.", [26,27,28,31,32,33]),
 ("Why can sanctions and incentives both fail when a value is sacred in Dehghani and colleagues (2009, 2010)?", "Both try to change the material consequences, and for participants with a sacred value about nuclear energy, approval was lower and anticipated anger higher with the incentive and with sanctions than with the baseline.", [30,33]),
 ("How does the sacred-value account differ from expected utility theory in what a larger benefit does?", "Expected utility theory combines the value of outcomes with their chances, so a larger benefit should raise the expected utility. For moral absolutists in Ginges, Atran, Medin and Shikaki (2007), adding money increased anger, disgust and support for violence.", [18,28,29]),
]
